1
00:00:02,000 --> 00:00:06,404
The noise floor is what your receiver hears when there's nothing to hear.

2
00:00:06,404 --> 00:00:11,229
Tune to an empty channel and the display doesn't go to zero — there's a constant

3
00:00:11,229 --> 00:00:15,572
background level of random energy, thermal noise in the electronics plus

4
00:00:15,572 --> 00:00:18,106
environmental RF picked up by the antenna.

5
00:00:18,106 --> 00:00:19,916
That level is the noise floor.

6
00:00:19,916 --> 00:00:24,681
It's measured in dBm, and it sets the bar every signal must clear to be usable.

7
00:00:25,131 --> 00:00:27,528
The everyday anchor is a crowded room.

8
00:00:27,528 --> 00:00:32,636
The room is never silent — there's always a hum of conversation — and whether you

9
00:00:32,636 --> 00:00:37,871
hear someone depends not on how loudly they speak in absolute terms, but on whether

10
00:00:37,871 --> 00:00:39,448
they rise above that hum.

11
00:00:39,448 --> 00:00:44,683
Radio is the same: the floor is the hum, and "can I decode it" means "does it stand

12
00:00:44,683 --> 00:00:45,755
above the floor."

13
00:00:46,205 --> 00:00:48,706
The floor has a hard physical bottom.

14
00:00:48,706 --> 00:00:53,910
Any resistor — including an antenna's radiation resistance — at temperature T

15
00:00:53,910 --> 00:00:59,520
generates thermal noise power k-T-B: Boltzmann's constant, times temperature, times

16
00:00:59,520 --> 00:01:00,196
bandwidth.

17
00:01:00,196 --> 00:01:05,738
At room temperature that works out to about minus one hundred seventy-four dBm per

18
00:01:05,738 --> 00:01:06,144
hertz.

19
00:01:06,144 --> 00:01:11,416
Multiply by your bandwidth — add ten log B in dB — and you get the floor for a

20
00:01:11,416 --> 00:01:17,026
perfect receiver: roughly minus one twenty-one dBm in a twelve-and-a-half kilohertz

21
00:01:17,026 --> 00:01:20,811
channel, minus one fourteen in a hundred-kilohertz span.

22
00:01:20,811 --> 00:01:22,974
No receiver can hear below this.

23
00:01:22,974 --> 00:01:24,055
Physics says so.

24
00:01:24,505 --> 00:01:27,684
Two things push the real floor above that ideal.

25
00:01:27,684 --> 00:01:33,048
First, the receiver adds its own noise — its noise figure: a five-dB noise figure

26
00:01:33,048 --> 00:01:37,949
raises the floor five dB above the thermal limit, which is why a low-noise

27
00:01:37,949 --> 00:01:43,313
amplifier placed first in the chain matters so much — it sets the noise figure of

28
00:01:43,313 --> 00:01:44,638
everything after it.

29
00:01:44,638 --> 00:01:50,069
Second, the environment contributes: atmospheric and galactic noise, and above all

30
00:01:50,069 --> 00:01:54,969
man-made interference — switching power supplies, USB buses, LED lighting,

31
00:01:54,969 --> 00:01:56,691
Ethernet, solar inverters.

32
00:01:56,691 --> 00:02:02,056
In a noisy urban install, the environment, not the receiver, dominates the floor.

33
00:02:02,506 --> 00:02:06,767
And here's the part people miss: the floor moves with the measurement.

34
00:02:06,767 --> 00:02:11,636
Thermal noise scales with bandwidth, so halving the receive bandwidth lowers the

35
00:02:11,636 --> 00:02:15,897
noise power by three dB while a narrowband signal inside stays intact.

36
00:02:15,897 --> 00:02:20,219
That's exactly why narrow channel filters and matched filtering improve

37
00:02:20,219 --> 00:02:24,967
signal-to-noise — and why a wideband capture always shows a higher floor: it's

38
00:02:24,967 --> 00:02:27,219
integrating noise over more spectrum.

39
00:02:27,669 --> 00:02:30,666
You've been looking at the noise floor all along.

40
00:02:30,666 --> 00:02:35,559
In GopherTrunk's waterfall, it's the grass — that speckled, ever-present texture

41
00:02:35,559 --> 00:02:37,578
across the bottom of the display.

42
00:02:37,578 --> 00:02:41,737
Every signal you'll ever decode is a shape standing above the grass.

43
00:02:41,737 --> 00:02:45,774
And the grass hands you a free diagnostic: disconnect the antenna.

44
00:02:45,774 --> 00:02:50,790
If the displayed floor drops several dB, environmental noise coming in through the

45
00:02:50,790 --> 00:02:55,377
antenna is dominating — and the fix is at the install, not in the software.

46
00:02:55,827 --> 00:02:59,604
That makes lowering the floor the cheapest way to hear more.

47
00:02:59,604 --> 00:03:04,827
Put a low-noise preamp at the antenna, before cable loss degrades the noise figure.

48
00:03:04,827 --> 00:03:09,925
Cut environmental pickup — ferrite chokes, quality shielded cable, moving the SDR

49
00:03:09,925 --> 00:03:14,142
away from computers and chargers can drop the floor ten dB or more.

50
00:03:14,142 --> 00:03:16,534
Filter out strong out-of-band signals.

51
00:03:16,534 --> 00:03:21,695
And match the receive bandwidth to the signal, so noise isn't admitted needlessly.

52
00:03:21,695 --> 00:03:26,604
GopherTrunk cannot decode a signal sitting at or below the floor — getting the

53
00:03:26,604 --> 00:03:31,576
floor down is the highest-leverage move an operator has before touching any DSP

54
00:03:31,576 --> 00:03:32,079
setting.

55
00:03:32,529 --> 00:03:37,571
So: the noise floor is the ever-present hum — thermal physics at the bottom,

56
00:03:37,571 --> 00:03:42,811
receiver and environment stacked on top — it's the grass on your waterfall, and

57
00:03:42,811 --> 00:03:45,796
it's fixable at the antenna, not in software.

58
00:03:45,796 --> 00:03:47,587
Full write-up linked below.

